# MKSTEMP(3)

## NAME

mkstemp, mkstemps - make a unique file and open it

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int mkstemp(char *template);
int mkstemps(char *template, int suffixlen);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Mkstemp* replaces *template* by a unique file name, creates that file with mode 0600, and
returns a descriptor open on it for reading and writing. It returns -1 if no name can be found
or the file cannot be made. The template should look like a file name with six trailing X's,
which are replaced with the current process id and a unique letter, exactly as mktemp(3) does;
the template must be **writable** - a string literal will not do, this machine's literals
living in the read-only const segment.

*Mkstemps* is the same, except that the last *suffixlen* bytes of the template are a fixed
suffix and the run of X's ends just before them, so that **/tmp/ccXXXXXX.tac** varies in the
middle and keeps its extension. *Mkstemp(template)* is *mkstemps(template, 0)*. A negative
*suffixlen* or one longer than the template is EINVAL .

Both are POSIX's or BSD's, and neither v7's nor C11's. *Mktemp* hands back a name and leaves
the caller to open it; *mkstemp* opens it itself, which is the whole of the difference.

## SEE ALSO

mktemp(3), creat(2), open(2), fopen(3) for *tmpfile* and *tmpnam*

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/mkstemp.c

## BUGS

**The creation is not atomic on this machine.** POSIX obtains its guarantee from
**O_CREAT**|**O_EXCL**, and this kernel has neither (*<fcntl.h>*): creation is reachable only
through creat(2), which does not fail on a file that already exists. So *mkstemp* tests the
name with access(2) and creates it immediately afterwards, which is mktemp(3)'s own race and
not an improvement on it. What it does buy over *mktemp* is that the file exists, and is open
read-write, before the caller writes a byte - so nothing downstream has to open a name a second
time.

**It walks a single letter,** inheriting mktemp(3)'s reach: 27 names for a given process and no
more.
